   "newText": "The demo's source upper-right pattern was copied directly into left/bottom panels; this is alignment-preserving tiling, not mirror symmetry about the dividers. In the test, a right-side mark at column 4 mapped to column 1 in the left two-column panel because both correspond to local column 1 within their respective tiles.\n\n## Additional solved evidence: replicate one tile in both dimensions (task recurrence, 2026-09-29)\n\nA fresh instance with a 17x11 grid had divider columns 3 and 7 (color 4) and divider rows 5 and 11 (color 4), making a 3x3 arrangement of 5x3 panels. The 5x3 tile at rows 6–10, columns 0–2 contained background 1 plus marks: local `(row 0,col 2)=7`, `(row 1,col 0)=9`, `(row 2,col 1)=7`. The accepted output copied this entire tile, same orientation and local offsets, to every panel at row starts 0, 6, 12 and column starts 0, 4, 8, leaving divider rows/columns intact. This instance was solved after one incorrect submission.\n\nImportant correction: replication is two-dimensional across all matching panels, not only horizontally within the initially marked band. Do not infer a tile's full content from the grid's blank-looking panels; use the marked tile as the pattern and repeat it across panel rows and columns. The demo was 11x5 with divider row 3 and column 2 (color 2), 3x2 panels of size 3x2. Its source tile's non-background content was `[[6,6],[8,1],[1,1]]`; the output tiled that pattern in all six panels. This confirms whole-tile repetition over both axes."
